upportforpublicutilitycompaniesinadditiontocontinuedinvestmentsintorenewableenergyandglobalcollaboration.Althoughtheseeffortsavoidedacatastrophicglobalenergydilemma,someofthesemeasurespushedeconomicallychallengednations,especiallyintheMiddleEastandAsia,intoeconomichardshipwithastronomicalnationaldebt.Further,theUNSustainableDevelopmentGoalsincludeglobalobjectivesfocusedonpromotinguniversalaccesstoaffordableandsustainableenergy. Althoughthesegoalsdon’tspecificallytargetEurope,a2022UN-EnergyreportoutlineshowtheUNhasestablishedanenergycompactthat’smobilized$600billioninfundingforimprovingenergyaccessandatransitiontorenewablesinemergingnations.Thereportfurtheremphasizeshowsupportingthesecountriesisimperativetoslowingworldwideclimatechange.In2022,thePeople’sRepublicofChinarefrainedfromimposingdetrimentalsanctionsonRussia.Instead,Chinaincreaseditscrudeoilandgasimportswhileothernationsconversely\n2\nStorz,Natalia.“ANewEuropeanCommissionFacesThreeKeyIssuesattheHeartoftheCleanEnergyTransition.”AtlanticCouncil,8Oct.2024,www.atlanticcouncil.org/blogs/energysource/a-new-european-commission-faces-three-key-issues-at-the-heart-of-the-clean-energy-transition/\n1\nNortham,Jackie.“RussiaIsUsingEnergyasaWeapon.CouldThisSpreadtotheRestoftheWorld?”NPR.org,2Nov.2022,npr.org/2022/11/02/1133667483/russia-is-using-energy-as-a-weapon-could-this-spread-to-the-rest-of-the-world.lessenedtheirrelianceonRussianenergy. BetweenMarchandJulyof2022,theMoscowTimesreportedthatChinaimported$35billioninRussiangas,oil,andcoal,a75percentincreasefromthesameperiodin2021.\n3 \nAdditionally,thiscommitmenttoanenergypartnershipbetweenthesetwonationsisexemplifiedbynewpartnershipsbetweenBeijingandMoscowthatenabletheconstructionofadditionalnaturalgaspipelinesbetweenthecountries.\n4 \nInadditiontostrengtheningfossilfueltieswithRussia,Chinainvested$890billionintorenewableenergyinnovationandmanufacturing,especiallyinelectricvehicles,solarPV, andenergystoragesectors.Theinvestmentrepresenteda40%increasewhencomparedwith2